1 NOTE Shoemaker or boot maker. In Bury St Edmunds in 1841 and in Christ Chur
2 CONC ch, Southwark by 1861. In 1881 he and Mary were at the Holland Street 
2 CONC almshouses, almost certainly Hopton's Almshouses; he was described as 
2 CONC a shoemaker (almsman). His 1 March 1890 entry is in the Christ Church 
2 CONC Workhouse death register, with civil death registered in St Saviour ag
2 CONC ed 87. Parents not ascertained. The London copper-plate manufacturer J
2 CONC ohn Shafe (apprenticed 1794; died 1846) remains a research lead becaus
2 CONC e of the rare surname and because this John's son Charles was a copper
2 CONC -plate polisher. However, the manufacturer's will centres on three min
2 CONC or natural children by Jane Hewitt and supplies no adult son or Suffol
2 CONC k connection, so no pedigree link is justified. Search Shafe, Shave, S
2 CONC hape, Shaff, Shaffe, Sheaf, Schafe and Scafe in Bury, Debenham, Depden
2 CONC  and nearby registers, settlement and apprenticeship records. Correcti
2 CONC on: an earlier tree note wrongly attributed an 1820 larceny and transp
2 CONC ortation case to John; the defendant was Robert Shafe.
